.TH "OSMIUM-DIFF" "1" "1.19.1" "" ""
.SH NAME
osmium\-diff \- display differences between OSM files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]osmium diff\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]OSM\-FILE1\f[R]
\f[I]OSM\-FILE2\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Finds all differences between two OSM files and displays them.
This command compares all attributes of all objects, so it will even
find, say, differences in the user name or even the order of tags,
differences that should not happen in normal OSM data unless there is
also a different object version.
.PP
Only differences between objects (node, ways, and relations) are found
and displayed.
Headers are ignored.
.PP
Objects in both input files must be sorted in the same order.
.PP
Several output formats are supported, see the \f[B]OUTPUT FORMATS\f[R]
section.
.PP
This command is intended for displaying the differences between files to
humans.
It can not be used to create an OSM change file (\f[CR].osc\f[R]), use
\f[B]osmium\-derive\-changes\f[R] for that.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
The following output formats are supported and can be set with the
\f[B]\-\-output\-format/\-f\f[R] options.
Default is the compact format.
.TP
compact
A very compact format.
For all objects a line is printed with the type of object (`n', `w', or
`r'), the object ID and then the version number.
If objects appear in both files and are identical they are preceded by a
space (\(cq `) character, if they are in both files, but different, they
are preceded by an asterisk ('*`).
Otherwise they get a minus ('\-`) or plus ('+\(cq) character to show
that they are only in the first or second file, respectively.
.TP
opl
The usual OPL format with all lines preceded by space (\(cq `), minus
('\-`), or plus ('+\(cq) characters depending on whether the object is
in both, the first, or the second file.
.TP
debug
The usual debug format with all lines preceded by space (\(cq `), minus
('\-`), or plus ('+`) characters depending on whether the object is in
both, the first, or the second file.
Color support can be enabled (\(cqdebug,color').
.PP
None of the output formats print the headers of the input files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-c, \-\-suppress\-common
Do not output objects that are the same in both files.
.TP
\-f, \-\-output\-format=FORMAT
See the \f[B]OUTPUT FORMATS\f[R] section.
.TP
\-\-ignore\-changeset
Ignore changeset id on OSM objects when comparing files.
When used with the OPL output format the `changeset' attribute is not
written to the output.
.TP
\-\-ignore\-uid
Ignore user id on OSM objects when comparing files.
When used with the OPL output format the `uid' attribute is not written
to the output.
.TP
\-\-ignore\-user
Ignore user name on OSM objects when comparing files.
When used with the OPL output format the `user' attribute is not written
to the output.
.TP
\-o, \-\-output=FILE
Name of the output file.
Default is `\-' (STDOUT).
.TP
\-O, \-\-overwrite
Allow an existing output file to be overwritten.
Normally \f[B]osmium\f[R] will refuse to write over an existing file.
.TP
\-q, \-\-quiet
No output.
Just report when files differ through the return code.
.TP
\-s, \-\-summary
Print count of objects that are only in the left or right files, or the
same in both or different in both to STDERR.
.TP
\-t, \-\-object\-type=TYPE
Read only objects of given type (\f[I]node\f[R], \f[I]way\f[R],
\f[I]relation\f[R]).
By default all types are read.
This option can be given multiple times.
This affects the output as well as the return code of the command.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\-h, \-\-help
Show usage help.
.TP
\-v, \-\-verbose
Set verbose mode.
The program will output information about what it is doing to STDERR.
.TP
\-\-progress
Show progress bar.
Usually a progress bar is only displayed if STDOUT and STDERR are
detected to be TTY.
With this option a progress bar is always shown.
Note that a progress bar will never be shown when reading from STDIN or
a pipe.
.TP
\-\-no\-progress
Do not show progress bar.
Usually a progress bar is displayed if STDOUT and STDERR are detected to
be a TTY.
With this option the progress bar is suppressed.
Note that a progress bar will never be shown when reading from STDIN or
a pipe.
.SH INPUT OPTIONS
.TP
\-F, \-\-input\-format=FORMAT
The format of the input file(s).
Can be used to set the input format if it can\(cqt be autodetected from
the file name(s).
This will set the format for all input files, there is no way to set the
format for some input files only.
See \f[B]osmium\-file\-formats\f[R](5) or the libosmium manual for
details.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\f[B]osmium diff\f[R] exits with exit code
.TP
0
if the files are the same,
.TP
1
if the files are different, or
.TP
2
if there was an error
.SH MEMORY USAGE
\f[B]osmium diff\f[R] doesn\(cqt keep a lot of data in memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show difference between Nepal files from January 2016 and February 2016
in compact format:
.IP
.EX
osmium diff nepal\-20160101.osm.pbf nepal\-20160201.osm.pbf
.EE
.PP
Show in color debug format only those objects that are different:
.IP
.EX
osmium diff nepal\-20160101.osm.pbf nepal\-20160201.osm.pbf \-f debug,color \-c
.EE
